const body = `
<h2 id="eleven-years">Eleven Years of Walking a Little Less</h2>
<p>It started in 2014. I was 57, a school librarian, on my feet all day, hauling stacks of returns and kneeling at the bottom shelves for the little ones. I loved it.</p>
<p>Then one Tuesday in March, I bent to lift a box of books and couldn't straighten back up.</p>
<p>A hot wire ran from my lower back down the back of my left leg. My legs felt like they belonged to someone else.</p>
<p>Frank drove me to urgent care. They sent me for an MRI.</p>
<p><strong>"Worn, compressed discs at L4-L5 and L5-S1, pressing on the nerve."</strong></p>
${fig("/images/br/slot-a-mri.webp", "An MRI report letter on a kitchen table beside reading glasses and a coffee mug", 900, 506, "The letter that started the parade.")}
<p>I'd never heard those words. I learned them fast.</p>
<p>And here's what nobody warns you about. It doesn't take your life all at once. It takes it a few feet at a time.</p>
<p>Every few months, the walk got shorter. The loop around the mall. Then the grocery store without a cart. Then the end of the block. Then just the mailbox.</p>
<p>I was doing everything right.</p>
<p>And I was still losing ground.</p>

<h2 id="parade">The Parade</h2>
<p>What came next, I now call the parade. Everyone in a white coat got a turn.</p>
<div class="lbl">
<p><strong>Physical therapy:</strong> 12 weeks, three times a week. I cried in the car after most sessions. The exercises were correct. My back got worse anyway. The therapist finally said, "Some people just don't respond." I was one of them.</p>
<p><strong>The chiropractor:</strong> Twice a week for eight months. I'd feel wonderful for about two hours. By the time I pulled into my driveway, it was back. I did the math once: $4,100 for about 60 hours of relief.</p>
<p><strong>Epidural injections:</strong> Three in 18 months. The first gave me seven glorious weeks. The second, three. The third did nothing at all. They wanted to try a fourth. I said no.</p>
<p><strong>Gabapentin:</strong> Made me foggy. Put 18 pounds on me. I still had the pain. Now I had pain plus fog.</p>
<p><strong>The medicine cabinet:</strong> You know exactly what I mean.</p>
<p><strong>A $350 TENS unit my son ordered online:</strong> It tingled on my skin. Nothing deeper. It lived in a drawer after two weeks.</p>
<p><strong>A back brace:</strong> Sweaty. And when I took it off, my back felt weaker than before.</p>
<p><strong>An inversion table:</strong> Hanging upside down at 62. Terrifying. It hurt my hips and did nothing for my back. Frank sold it in the spring.</p>
<p><strong>Yoga:</strong> Every morning for a year. I got flexible everywhere except the one place that hurt.</p>
</div>
<p>By 2021, I was scheduled for a fusion.</p>
<p>My surgeon, a good man, I still believe that, said it had "a very good chance" of fixing the pain.</p>
<p>I had the surgery in March 2022.</p>
<p>I woke up hopeful.</p>
<p>For about three months.</p>

<h2 id="failed">The Morning I Knew It Hadn't Worked</h2>
<p>It was a Saturday in July. Five months after the operation.</p>
<p>I'd been sleeping in the recliner because I couldn't get comfortable in our bed.</p>
<p>I tried to stand up.</p>
<p>I couldn't.</p>
<p>The same concrete in my lower back. The same hot wire down my leg. The same panic I'd felt that Tuesday in 2014.</p>
${fig("/images/br/slot-a-recliner.webp", "An empty recliner with a folded blanket in a dim living room at dawn", 900, 506, "Where I slept for most of 2022.")}
<p>I sat on the edge of that recliner and cried harder than I had in years.</p>
<p>Not from the pain. From the math.</p>
<p>I had tried everything. And I was right back where I started.</p>
<p>Actually, no. I was worse. Now I had hardware. Scar tissue. Fewer options. And at my six-month follow-up, my surgeon said: "Ruth, the fusion looks good on the X-ray. Sometimes the pain doesn't follow the pictures. This may be your new normal."</p>
<p>My new normal. At 65.</p>
<p>That's when I started giving things away.</p>

<h2 id="question">The Question Nobody Had Asked in Eleven Years</h2>
<p>My son-in-law Dale is not a doctor.</p>
<p>He has rebuilt truck suspensions for 30 years.</p>
<p>Two Thanksgivings ago, after the dishes were done and everyone else had gone to bed, he watched me lower myself into a kitchen chair like I was getting into a cold lake.</p>
<p>Then he asked me a question.</p>
<p><strong>"Ruth, in eleven years, has anybody ever tried to take the weight off those discs? Not numb them. Not cut them. Not bolt them together. Just unload them?"</strong></p>
<p>I opened my mouth. Then I closed it.</p>
<p>A dozen specialists. Two MRIs. Physical therapy. A surgery where they opened up my spine.</p>
<p>Not one of them had treated the compression itself.</p>
<p>They treated the pain. They treated the inflammation. They fused two bones.</p>
<p>Nobody ever tried to give my discs their room back.</p>

<h2 id="shock-absorbers">What a Truck Mechanic Knew That My Specialists Never Mentioned</h2>
<p>Dale drew it on a paper napkin.</p>
${fig("/images/br/slot-a-napkin.webp", "A ballpoint sketch on a paper napkin of a truck shock absorber next to a stack of spine bones", 900, 506, "Dale's napkin. I still have it.")}
<p>"Your discs are shock absorbers," he said. "Same as on a truck."</p>
<p>Between every pair of bones in your spine sits a cushion. And that cushion is mostly water.</p>
<p>"A shock that gets squeezed for years loses its fluid," Dale said. "It goes flat. And a flat shock sends every bump straight into the frame."</p>
<p>In your back, the frame is your nerves.</p>
${fig("/images/br/slot-a-spine.webp", "Sketch of a lower spine with flattened discs between the vertebrae and a nerve line highlighted in red", 900, 506, "Flat shocks: the cushions between the bones lose their water, and the space for the nerve closes.")}
<p>I'm a librarian. So the next morning, I did what librarians do. I looked it up.</p>

<h2 id="taller">You Are Taller in the Morning Than at Night</h2>
<p>The first thing I found, I could test in my own hallway.</p>
<p>All day long, the weight of your body squeezes water out of your discs. All night, lying flat, they soak some of it back up.</p>
<p>That's why you are measurably taller when you wake up than when you go to bed, by about half an inch for most adults. Researchers have measured disc fluid rising and falling with posture since the 1980s.</p>
<p>Frank and I tried it. A pencil mark on the pantry doorframe at 7 a.m. Another at 9 p.m.</p>
<p>I lost almost half an inch by bedtime.</p>
${fig("/images/br/slot-a-doorframe.webp", "Two pencil marks on a pantry doorframe, labelled 7 a.m. and 9 p.m., the evening mark slightly lower", 900, 506, "Our pantry doorframe. Morning mark on top.")}
<p>That half inch is your discs giving up water under load and drinking it back when the load comes off.</p>
<p>Now here's the cruel part.</p>
<p>When you're young, the overnight refill keeps up. But the company behind the device I use now puts it plainly: after about age 30, your discs start to dry out and shrink.</p>
<p>The night isn't long enough anymore. Every year, you get back a little less than you lost.</p>
<p>The cushions flatten. The bones settle closer together. The space your nerves run through gets tighter.</p>
<p>That's why I walked a little less every few months.</p>
<p>Not my age. Not my willpower.</p>
<p><strong>Compression, piling up a little more every season, and nobody ever taking it off.</strong></p>

<h2 id="guarding">Why Your Back Muscles Lock the Squeeze in Place</h2>
<p>The second thing I found explained the parade.</p>
<p>When a spine is squeezed and a nerve is pinched, the muscles around it do something protective. They clamp down. Doctors call it guarding. It's a reflex. You don't choose it and you can't switch it off.</p>
<p>It's why every stretch, every adjustment, every massage felt wonderful for an hour or two and then snapped back like a rubber band.</p>
<p>The chiropractor pushed my bones into place. My clamped-down muscles pulled them right back before I got home.</p>
<p>Put those two together (discs squeezed dry, and muscles clamped around them so nothing can open up) and you get what I've come to call <strong>Compression Lock</strong>.</p>
<p>It doesn't let go on its own.</p>
<p>Not with rest. Not with stretching. Not with pills. Not with yoga, swimming or any "core program" on earth.</p>
<p>Because my back wasn't weak.</p>
<p><strong>It was locked down under pressure.</strong></p>

<h2 id="why-failed">Why Everything I Tried Had Failed</h2>
<p>Sitting at that kitchen table at 11 p.m. on Thanksgiving night, the whole parade finally made sense.</p>
<div class="lbl">
<p><strong>Physical therapy</strong> tried to strengthen the muscles around a spine that was still being crushed. You can't exercise your way out of a squeeze.</p>
<p><strong>The injections</strong> calmed the inflammation for a few weeks. The compression underneath never moved an inch.</p>
<p><strong>The TENS unit</strong> buzzed my skin. It never took one ounce of pressure off one disc.</p>
<p><strong>The chiropractor</strong> pushed my bones. My guarding muscles pulled them back by lunchtime.</p>
<p><strong>The inversion table</strong> stretched me while every muscle in my back clenched like a fist from fear.</p>
<p><strong>The pills</strong> muted the alarm while the fire kept burning.</p>
<p><strong>The surgery</strong> fused two bones. The discs above and below were still being squeezed dry every single day.</p>
</div>
<p>I wasn't broken.</p>
<p>I wasn't "one of the ones who don't respond."</p>
<p>I had eleven years of compression on my spine, and nobody had ever taken it off.</p>

<h2 id="unload-refill-release">The Only Thing That Opens It Back Up</h2>
<p>Dale's point was simple. A flat shock doesn't refill while it's still under load. You have to take the load off, and you have to give it something to drink.</p>
<p>It takes three things, at the same time:</p>
<div class="parts">
<p><strong>1. UNLOAD.</strong> Gently pull the bones of the lower spine apart. In clinic research published in 1994, researchers placed a pressure sensor inside patients' discs during spinal decompression and watched the pressure drop below zero. A vacuum. That vacuum is what pulls fluid back toward the disc instead of squeezing it out.</p>
<p><strong>2. REFILL.</strong> Add heat. Warmth opens the blood vessels around the spine and floods the area with blood flow. Cold muscles fight a stretch. Warm ones let go. As the device maker puts it, stretching without heat is "pulling on cold rubber."</p>
<p><strong>3. RELEASE.</strong> Add vibration. Steady vibration calms the guarding reflex, so the muscles stop snapping everything back and the space you open stays open.</p>
</div>
<p>Miss one and it doesn't hold. Stretch without heat and vibration, and you get my inversion table. Heat alone is a heating pad: lovely, and it takes no pressure off. Massage alone is my chiropractor's two hours.</p>
<p>Clinics do this on decompression tables. One owner wrote that he was paying "$145 a session just to get traction on my lower back." Then he found a device that "gives me that exact same decompression at home, for less than the cost of two visits."</p>
